{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 14,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.641,
            0.049,
            0.847,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Finding Common Ground"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.089,
            0.847,
            0.167
          ],
          "content": "This is similar to the procedure for prime factorisation. At each step, the two numbers are divided by a common prime factor, and the two quotients are written down in the next row. This continues till we get two numbers that do not have any common prime factors."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.175,
            0.724,
            0.198
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "How do we use this to find the HCF of 84 and 180? Explore."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.206,
            0.846,
            0.246
          ],
          "content": "[**Hint:** Observe that $8 4 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 7$ , and $180 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 15$ similar to prime factorisation]"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.254,
            0.502,
            0.278
          ],
          "content": "Find the HCF in the following cases."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.27,
            0.284,
            0.417,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{c|cccc}\\overbrace{2} & 300,150 &  &  \\\\\\overbrace{5} & 150,75 &  &  \\\\\\underbrace{5} & \\underbrace{30,15} &  &  \\\\\\underbrace{3} & \\underbrace{6,3} &  &  \\\\& 2,1 &  &  \\\\\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.589,
            0.284,
            0.726,
            0.386
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{c|c}\\widehat{2} & 630, 770 \\\\\\hline5 & 315, 385 \\\\\\hline7 & 63, 77 \\\\\\hline& 9, 11 \\\\\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.243,
            0.424,
            0.423,
            0.443
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{HCF} = 2 \\times 5 \\times 5 \\times 3",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.59,
            0.424,
            0.738,
            0.443
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{HCF} = 2 \\times 5 \\times 7",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.453,
            0.846,
            0.492
          ],
          "content": "This procedure not only gives the HCF but can also be used to find the LCM! Can you see how?"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.259,
            0.499,
            0.408,
            0.627
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{c|ccc}\\widehat{2} & \\widehat{300,150} &  \\\\\\hline5 & \\widehat{150,75} &  \\\\\\hline5 & \\widehat{30,15} &  \\\\\\hline3 & \\widehat{5,3} &  \\\\\\hline2,1 &  \\\\\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.575,
            0.5,
            0.727,
            0.627
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{c|ccc}\\widehat{2} & 630, 770 &  \\\\\\hline5 & 315, 385 &  \\\\\\hline7 & 63, 77 &  \\\\\\hline3 & \\underbrace{9, 11}_{3, 11} &  \\\\\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.642,
            0.457,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{LCM}=2 \\times 5 \\times 5 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.524,
            0.642,
            0.783,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{LCM}=2 \\times 5 \\times 7 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 11",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.675,
            0.402,
            0.699
          ],
          "content": "Why are these the LCMs?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.702,
            0.847,
            0.758
          ],
          "content": "[**Hint:** Will the product of the factors marked as the LCM of 300 and 150 contain the prime factorisations of both 300 and 150? Is this the smallest such number?]"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.759,
            0.254,
            0.902
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_image_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.759,
            0.846,
            0.798
          ],
          "content": "Guna says “I found a better way to factorise to find HCF/LCM. This is faster than what was taught in class!”"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.798,
            0.846,
            0.834
          ],
          "content": "“For the numbers 300 and 150, I can first directly divide both numbers by 50."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.294,
            0.836,
            0.518,
            0.855
          ],
          "content": "The HCF will be 50 × 3."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.648,
            0.823,
            0.775,
            0.894
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{c|ccc}50 & 300, 150 \\\\\\hline3 & 6, & 3 \\\\\\hline& 2, & 1 \\\\\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.813,
            0.934,
            0.836,
            0.949
          ],
          "content": "61"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
